{
  "about": "The bytes a person's key signs FOR A SERVER (2026-11-20; the audience line 2026-11-21). A REQUEST is signed over the domain line, the audience, the purpose and the time in milliseconds, joined by a newline; a SIGN-IN (the answer to a relay's identify challenge) over the identify domain line, the audience, the relay's nonce and the person's key. The audience is the address the app connected to: its host name, lowercase, and its port when that is not the scheme's default (audiences: the URL an app opened and the audience it signs for, null when none; since round 3 an app reads the URL twice, by hand and by the WHATWG URL parser it sends with, and signs nothing when the two differ: a backslash, a trailing dot, an IPv4 or IPv6 address written any but its one canonical way). Chat messages are signed over text then a newline then the time, so a chat text that starts with a signing domain line (hum/<name>/v<n> then a newline) is refused by every chat signer and by the relay: chat_refused must be refused and chat_allowed must not. Held to by crates/humanity-protocol/src/signed_request.rs (request_preimage, identify_preimage, audience_of, starts_with_signing_domain), src/relay/signed_request.rs, src/net/identity.rs, and the website's web/chat/pq.js (pqRequestPreimage, pqIdentifyPreimage, pqAudienceOf, pqIsReservedChatText) through scripts/tests/signed-request-web.test.js.",
